#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""heimdall-fallback -- quota-exhaustion fallback POLICY gate (state, config,
and the safety gate only -- no transport).

WHY THIS EXISTS
---------------
When Claude capacity is exhausted mid-task, hmd may -- ONLY if the operator
opts in -- route work to a locally-hosted OmniRoute gateway using the
OPERATOR'S OWN third-party API keys. See
docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md for the full risk analysis
(verdict: do NOT integrate directly; every constraint below is sourced from
that doc, none invented here). The Tier-1 boundary specifically is enforced
per docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md's positive-
verification design (see run_preflight): there is no config flag anywhere in
OmniRoute that disables Tier-1, so this tool verifies the real structural
control -- credential ABSENCE -- instead of trusting a self-attested boolean.

THIS TOOL IS POLICY ONLY. It computes state, config, and the safety-gate
verdict. It does NOT implement transport -- it never calls OmniRoute, never
rewrites a request, never speaks to anything but its own local reachability
probe and OmniRoute's own SQLite DB (read-only). The seam a future transport
layer must consult is `check`'s exit code. Nothing else in this repo may
treat "the operator flipped a config flag" as license to skip this gate.

THE SAFETY BOUNDARY (mirrors bin/heimdall-quota-advisor's own header -- that
tool prints options for a human; this one gates an unattended fallback, same
boundary from a different angle):
  1. NEVER enable, configure, or route through OmniRoute's "Tier 1
     Subscription (Claude Code, Codex, Copilot)" mode -- it repurposes a
     Claude Code / Codex / Copilot subscription's own auth as a backend for
     arbitrary routed traffic (assessment doc, "Tier 1 Subscription"
     section). There is no config flag that disables Tier-1 -- none exists in
     OmniRoute to positively verify (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-
     credential-isolation.md, S6) -- so this tool instead positively verifies
     the actual structural control, credential ABSENCE, across four
     independent checks, ALL required, none short-circuiting another:
       a. tier1_credential_absent -- no `provider_connections` row for
          provider 'claude' or 'claude-web' in OmniRoute's own SQLite DB (the
          real gate; S2-S3). An absent or unreadable DB is NOT a pass -- it
          is a FAIL, because absence-of-evidence is not evidence-of-absence
          for an externally-owned database this tool does not control.
       b. anthropic_model_pinned -- ANTHROPIC_MODEL must be set AND carry an
          explicit `provider/` prefix. Claude Code emits bare `claude-*` ids
          when this is unset, and OmniRoute has an explicit routing branch
          that matches a bare `claude-*` id to provider `claude` (S5). The
          pin is the second line of defense behind (a), never a substitute
          for it.
       c. prefer_claude_code_flag_off --
          OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS must stay
          unset/false. Default is false; this is a Tier-1 WIDENING knob, the
          opposite polarity from what an operator would expect of a variable
          with "prefer" in its name (S3, "must not do" #6).
       d. no_delegated_sidecar -- no delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI, or
          Dario -- OmniRoute's own source names it a "Claude-subscription
          proxy") may be configured. A `mode` of 'cliproxyapi', 'dario', or
          'fallback' on an upstream_proxy_config row can reach a Claude
          subscription while OmniRoute's own provider_connections table
          holds no Tier-1 token at all, which would make check (a) alone
          insufficient (S1a path 8, residual risk 2). Confirmed against a
          live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682) install that this lives on
          upstream_proxy_config, NOT provider_connections (which has no
          `mode` column at all) -- see SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES. An
          upstream_proxy_config table that does not exist AT ALL is a PASS,
          never a FAIL, when provider_connections (check (a), just above) is
          itself readable -- a schema predating migration
          138_dario_fallback_backend.sql structurally cannot have a
          delegated sidecar, which is a stronger, positive fact than
          "unknown". Genuine unreadability (provider_connections ALSO
          failing) still fails closed exactly as every other branch here
          does -- see run_preflight's own comment for the full reasoning,
          and test/issue-loop-claude-fix-fallback.test.sh's case (c) for the
          real caller whose own hermetic fixture is exactly this DB shape.
     Fail closed on all four: refusing is always safer than proceeding on an
     unknown.
  2. NEVER enable OmniRoute's opt-in root-CA MITM ("TPROXY") decrypt mode. A
     config that mentions it in any form (see FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS) is
     treated as corrupt and forced back to state=off.
  3. NEVER route on OmniRoute's own ToS-conflict flag alone. OmniRoute's docs
     call that flag advisory, not a routing gate, and still route through
     flagged providers by default. hmd applies its OWN gate instead:
     target_provider_allowed refuses any provider named in config's
     `tos_flagged_providers`, added to a BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS
     starting set that is EMPTY by design (owner decision, 2026-08-26 -- see
     that set's own comment for the full reasoning: the prior ~30-entry set
     was sourced from the assessment doc's Section 2, a table built for
     OmniRoute's free-tier POOLED-credential routing, not this tool's
     operator's-own-key model, and copying it verbatim left the fallback
     feature permanently inert). Config may still only ADD to this set,
     never remove or override an entry that IS present -- an operator who
     wants a provider denied still names it in `tos_flagged_providers`.
  4. Operator's own API keys ONLY. This tool never reads, reuses, or forwards
     the Claude Code OAuth session -- it only checks whether an
     operator-named env var is SET (see run_preflight's "operator_key"
     check). It never reads the value, and an operator_key_env that names a
     Claude/Anthropic credential is itself a preflight failure (see
     FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS).
  5. NEVER print or log a secret. Every subcommand reports key PRESENCE
     ("configured" / "absent") only, never a value. Follows the non-TTY
     secret-refusal precedent in bin/heimdall-invite (secrets withheld by
     default, never inferred from an ambient variable of the same name) --
     this tool goes further still: it has no print-the-secret override at
     all, because it never has a reason to print one.
  6. `off` is the default for a fresh install AND for any absent or corrupt
     config. A corrupt config must never be silently read as anything but
     `off` -- see load_config()'s fail-closed parse.
  7. NEVER treat OmniRoute's `blockedProviders` setting as evidence of
     anything. Its own UI copy is "Hide specific providers from the
     /v1/models response" -- it is consulted only in model-listing/search
     paths and is never read by getProviderCredentials() (S3 point 1, S6). A
     same-named key placed in THIS tool's own config is already dropped
     silently by load_config()'s allowlist copy; cmd_check additionally
     warns on stderr if it finds one, so that silence is never mistaken for
     the field having done something.
  8. FOUR STATES, one safety boundary. `off` (default, never route), `auto`
     (the exhaustion/pre-exhaustion reaction -- route only when preflight
     passes AND heimdall-session-usage confirms the ~95% threshold is
     crossed, else WAIT rather than block -- see point 11), `switch`
     (route EVERYTHING, tier-blind, still gated by the same preflight as
     every other state), and `coop` (a deliberate, standing, PER-ROLE
     allowlist -- never quota-reactive, never WAITs; see point 13). None of
     the four ever changes WHETHER routing is safe -- points 1-7 above apply
     identically in all four; each state only changes WHAT routes and WHEN,
     never whether a failing Tier-1/model-pin/sidecar/ToS check is
     survivable.

     REMOVED (owner directive, 2026-08-28): a fourth state, `on`, used to
     exist as a CAPABILITY-TIER routing decision -- haiku-tier work only, via
     `check --tier T` -- entirely independent of exhaustion. That was a
     genuine, distinct capability, not a switch/auto near-duplicate: no
     surviving state offers a tier-restricted routing option, so this
     capability is gone, not renamed. `set on` / `arm --state on` are now
     REJECTED outright (argparse choices), naming the three valid states. A
     persisted `state: "on"` from before this change migrates to `off` on
     load -- never `auto` -- since `auto` is tier-blind and could grant MORE
     routing than `on`'s haiku-tier-only gate ever did (see load_config()).
  9. `switch` MUST be unmistakable. It is the only state that risks
     quality-sensitive work landing on a provider with no no-train guarantee
     (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-headroom-inpath-measurement.md's 337x
     prompt-cache-vs-compression gap is why routing HEALTHY traffic through
     OmniRoute is a real cost, not a free option). `status` and `check` both
     print an explicit, impossible-to-miss warning whenever state is
     'switch' -- never folded quietly into the normal state line.
  10. A no-auth/free target_provider needs NO operator_key_env, and that is
      NOT a gap -- it mirrors OmniRoute's own architecture. Credential
      resolution reads provider_connections for every provider EXCEPT a
      fixed set served via a synthetic "noauth" connection instead
      (NOAUTH_PROVIDERS, sourced verbatim from docs/analysis/2026-08-25-
      omniroute-credential-isolation.md's own citation of OmniRoute source;
      `claude`/`claude-web` are explicitly NOT in it, so this can never
      collide with point 1's Tier-1 boundary). The operator_key check passes
      keylessly ONLY for a target_provider in that list, or one the operator
      has explicitly named in config's `noauth_providers` -- an
      addition-only declaration for a provider this tool's hardcoded list
      has not caught up with yet, never a guess this tool makes on its own.
      Two things stay true regardless: (a) even a no-auth provider still
      refuses an operator_key_env naming a Claude/Anthropic credential
      (point 4 is absolute, not conditioned on necessity), and (b)
      target_provider_allowed's OWN ToS deny-list check is completely
      independent -- a provider can be no-auth AND still operator-denied
      (e.g. an operator who lists `duckduckgo-web` in `tos_flagged_providers`
      still gets it refused even though it is also in NOAUTH_PROVIDERS --
      see test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh). Keyless routes are OmniRoute's
      free/no-auth tier, the SAME weakest-contractual-terms category the
      ToS-conflict flag in point 3 exists for
      (docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md) -- `status` and
      `check` both say this once, plainly, whenever it applies, so the
      operator sees it without having to remember it.

  11. state=="auto" is the one state that is actually exhaustion-gated.
      It best-effort-consults bin/heimdall-session-usage's own
      ~95%-of-session-limit pre-exhaustion verdict and treats it as a
      SECOND, NECESSARY condition for ROUTE: `check` prints VERDICT: ROUTE
      under auto only when the preflight passes AND heimdall-session-usage
      reports "crossed". A passing preflight with capacity still available
      (verdict "under") WAITs instead of routing -- there is no reason to
      route off Anthropic while quota remains, and doing so anyway is pure
      cost/latency loss (cache is 91-95% of the value and OmniRoute drops
      cache_control; see
      docs/analysis/2026-08-25-headroom-inpath-measurement.md). This is a
      NECESSARY condition ADDED to the existing gate, never a substitute for
      it: crossing 95% still authorizes nothing on its own -- every
      preflight check above (Tier-1, model pin, sidecar, provider
      allow-list, all of it) still has to pass first, exactly as before; a
      failing preflight WAITs regardless of session_usage, crossed or not.

      heimdall-session-usage's own verdict is one of four: "unconfigured" /
      "unknown" / "under" / "crossed" -- all four are load-bearing and kept
      textually distinct on purpose. ROUTE requires an explicit `verdict ==
      "crossed"` allow-list match, never a `verdict != "under"` deny-list --
      so "unconfigured" and "unknown" can never be silently promoted into
      looking like a confirmed threshold-crossing. "unknown" (tool missing,
      timed out, or returned something this cannot parse) is deliberately
      treated the SAME as "under" for the routing decision -- both WAIT --
      but for a DIFFERENT, explicitly reasoned cause (see the dedicated
      `session_verdict == "unknown"` branch in _verdict()): when capacity
      cannot be measured at all, routing off Anthropic on the strength of a
      signal that was never confirmed would be exactly the fail-OPEN
      behavior this file exists to avoid (see "MUST FAIL CLOSED" below, and
      tier1_credential_absent / no_delegated_sidecar's identical "absence is
      not proof" reasoning). Waiting on "unknown" risks a delayed fallback
      if the measurement stays broken; routing on "unknown" risks burning
      fallback-provider capacity, and a cold/degraded cache, while Anthropic
      quota may still have been available -- this file already fails closed
      everywhere else, so it fails closed here too. "unknown" is NEVER read
      as "under" -- it stays its own reasoned branch with its own [INFO]
      wording; the two simply arrive at the same WAIT outcome by separate,
      explicit paths, not by being conflated into one. Best effort, no
      exceptions: a missing, non-executable, timed-out or malformed
      heimdall-session-usage collapses to "unknown" and changes nothing else
      about the OTHER checks -- see _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict().

  12. `arm` (heimdall-fallback arm [--provider P] [--state auto|switch])
      exists so "turn fallback on" does not mean "hand the operator a
      hand-edited JSON file and a checklist" -- see cmd_arm. It
      self-provisions everything it can HONESTLY justify: a target_provider
      (auto-picking a no-auth one from NOAUTH_PROVIDERS when nothing usable
      is already configured, reusing an already-valid existing one, or
      validating an operator-supplied --provider) and the matching
      operator_key_env (always empty for a no-auth target; for a keyed
      target, REUSED from an already-configured, already-populated,
      non-Claude env var -- never invented) -- then writes state. It obeys
      every constraint above with no exception carved out for itself: it
      refuses outright, and leaves the on-disk config byte-for-byte
      UNCHANGED, for a claude/claude-web target (point 1 -- arm enforces
      this itself, since target_provider is never cross-checked against
      TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS anywhere else in this file; the Tier-1 boundary is
      enforced by reading OmniRoute's own DB, not by inspecting this
      field), a ToS-denied target (point 3), a keyed target with no usable
      key (point 4 -- arm never invents a credential), or when no honest
      no-auth candidate remains. It cannot perform the two genuinely
      unavoidable manual steps -- exporting ANTHROPIC_MODEL into the
      PARENT shell (no child process can mutate the environment of the
      process that launched it; there is no syscall for that) and starting
      the OmniRoute gateway itself (bin/heimdall-fallback is a policy gate,
      never a transport -- see the module's opening line -- so starting a
      long-running service is squarely someone else's job) -- so it prints
      the exact `export` line and the gateway's real reachability instead
      of pretending either is already done. It always ends by calling the
      exact same run_preflight/_verdict path `check` uses, against
      whatever is now on disk, and returns that exact exit code -- arm is
      self-verifying, never a "trust me" tool.

  13. `coop` is deliberate, per-role, standing division of labour -- NOT a
      quota-reactive state like `auto`/`switch`. The MAIN agent (the
      operator's own Claude Code / Cursor session) NEVER routes under
      `coop` and stays on api.anthropic.com unconditionally -- that is
      coop's defining property, and the reason it cannot be implemented by
      simply widening `switch`'s allowlist. Only SPECIFIC named subagent
      roles the operator has explicitly opted in via `coop add <role>`
      route, and only because the operator chose to divide labour that
      way, never because a quota threshold tripped. Default allowlist is
      EMPTY: `coop` with no roles configured routes NOTHING -- the
      operator names roles in one at a time, never a guessed or inherited
      default set (see cmd_coop_add). Judgment roles
      (bin/lib/hmd-adjudication-set.sh's hmd_is_adjudication -- reviewer,
      verifier, security-auditor, incident-responder, plus any
      *-review*/*-audit*/*-verif* type) can NEVER be added to the
      allowlist -- refused at CONFIG TIME (cmd_coop_add), with a clear
      reason, not merely skipped later at check time; run_preflight's own
      coop_role_allowed check re-verifies this independently at
      check/route time too, so a hand-edited config that smuggled an
      adjudication role onto disk still cannot route it (defense in
      depth, not a single gate). Role resolution for `check` is
      `--role ROLE`, falling back to the HMD_AGENT_TYPE environment
      variable when --role is omitted -- the same variable
      bin/lib/hmd-route-claude already reads for its own judgment pin, and
      the only channel available at all for `base-url` (bin/heimdall-route
      calls it with no --role argument, and this file must never require
      that caller to change). A bare `check` under `coop` with no
      resolvable role (no --role, no HMD_AGENT_TYPE) reports REFUSE, never
      a misleading global ROUTE -- there is no such thing as "coop routes
      by default" for an unidentified caller. `coop` never sets a
      session-wide ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL and never can: cmd_base_url only
      ever prints an endpoint for THIS invocation's resolved role, and the
      main session's own launch never calls through this per-role path at
      all -- routing the main agent would destroy the entire point of the
      state. `coop` NEVER WAITs (mirrors `switch`'s always-decide shape,
      not `auto`'s exhaustion-gated one) -- a role is either on the
      allowlist and preflight-clean (ROUTE) or it is not (REFUSE); there
      is no "capacity not yet crossed" concept for a standing per-role
      division of labour. See run_preflight's `coop_role_allowed` check
      and _coop_role_allowed() for the exact allow/deny logic, and
      bin/heimdall-precheck-agent's own coop section for why an in-process
      Agent-tool spawn (no exec boundary) can never actually be routed
      even for an allowlisted role, and is refused outright rather than
      silently left on Anthropic while a caller believes it was routed.

MUST FAIL CLOSED -- the deliberate inverse of heimdall-quota-advisor's "MUST
FAIL OPEN". The advisor's job is to never cry wolf: an uncertain exhaustion
signal must stay silent (exit quietly, print nothing). This tool's job is
the opposite: an uncertain SAFETY signal must never say "safe to route". Any
internal error, missing field, or unparsable config resolves to the LEAST
permissive verdict available for the current state (REFUSE under off/switch,
WAIT under auto) -- never to ROUTE. Two inverse philosophies for two
different problems, not a contradiction: one gates a print statement, the
other gates unattended, keyed network egress.

NO AUTO-SWITCH CONTRADICTION. heimdall-quota-advisor documents that it
"never auto-switches ANY provider for the operator. Ever." This tool's
`auto`/`switch` states do not contradict that: (a) `off` is the default
everywhere, so a fresh install has zero auto-switch behavior anywhere in
this repo; (b) `auto`/`switch` are reachable ONLY via an explicit
`heimdall-fallback set auto|switch` the operator runs themselves -- hmd
never flips this on its own; (c) this tool does not even perform a switch --
it only computes whether a future transport layer would be ALLOWED to.

EXHAUSTION vs. OVERLOAD -- NOT THE SAME THING, NOT HANDLED HERE. A transient
529 / overloaded_error / 429 is a RETRYABLE condition with its own mechanism
(exponential backoff in bin/lib/hmd-claude-retry.sh, feeding the AIMD spawn
throttle in bin/lib/pressure_control.py). Genuine quota/session exhaustion is
a different condition entirely (classified by bin/lib/quota_stop.py,
surfaced via bin/heimdall-quota-advisor). This tool concerns ONLY the
latter. It has no opinion on retries and must never be consulted for one.

USAGE
-----
  heimdall-fallback status [--json]              current state + would-preflight-pass, secret-free
  heimdall-fallback set {off,auto,switch,coop}   persist a new state (off is always safe)
  heimdall-fallback check [--role ROLE]          run the preflight; exit code IS the verdict.
                                                  --role only matters under state=coop (falls
                                                  back to $HMD_AGENT_TYPE when omitted); ignored
                                                  under off/auto/switch.
  heimdall-fallback coop add ROLE                add/remove ROLE from the coop allowlist (add is
                        remove ROLE                 refused for an adjudication role -- header
                        list                        point 13) / print the current allowlist
  heimdall-fallback arm [--provider P]            self-provision target_provider +
                        [--state auto|switch]       operator_key_env (never inventing a
                                                  credential) and write state, then run
                                                  check and report its verdict. See header
                                                  point 12 (coop is deliberately not an
                                                  arm --state choice -- see point 13).
  heimdall-fallback where                        print the config file path

Global flags (before the subcommand -- matches bin/heimdall-pressure /
bin/heimdall-metric):
  --repo PATH   repo root containing .heimdall/ (default: .)
  --strict      tests only; callers never pass this

CONFIG  <repo>/.heimdall/fallback.json -- gitignored by the blanket
`.heimdall/*` rule in .gitignore; no committed .example needed since every
field below defaults to the safe value:
  {
    "schema": 1,
    "state": "off",                        # off | auto | switch | coop -- anything else,
                                            # including the removed 'on', reads as off
                                            # (header point 6)
    "operator_key_env": "",                # NAME of an env var holding the operator's
                                            # OWN OmniRoute/provider key -- never a value.
                                            # NOT required when target_provider is a
                                            # no-auth/free provider -- see header point 10
    "endpoint": "http://127.0.0.1:20128",  # must resolve local; OmniRoute's documented
                                            # default self-hosted gateway port
    "omniroute_db_path": "",               # path to OmniRoute's own SQLite DB; empty ->
                                            # $DATA_DIR/storage.sqlite or ~/.omniroute/
                                            # storage.sqlite (positive Tier-1 credential
                                            # check -- see header point 1a)
    "cliproxyapi_dir": "",                 # path to CLIProxyAPI's config dir; empty ->
                                            # $CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR or ~/.cli-proxy-api
                                            # (delegated-sidecar check -- header point 1d)
    "target_provider": "",                 # provider name; checked against the deny list
    "tos_flagged_providers": [],           # operator ADDITIONS to the built-in deny
                                            # list only -- can never remove a built-in
    "noauth_providers": [],                # operator ADDITIONS to the built-in no-auth
                                            # (keyless) provider list only -- see header
                                            # point 10; never removes a built-in entry
    "coop_roles": []                       # explicit per-role allowlist for state=coop,
                                            # EMPTY BY DEFAULT -- an operator names roles
                                            # in one at a time via `coop add`; an
                                            # adjudication role can never be added (see
                                            # header point 13)
  }

EXIT CODES
  status, where : always 0 (never-fail-caller telemetry reads)
  set           : 0 (even on bad input) unless --strict -> 2 usage / 3 write-fail
  check         : 0 = ROUTE  (every preflight check passed; under state=auto,
                              heimdall-session-usage must also report "crossed";
                              under state=coop, the resolved role must also be
                              on the coop allowlist -- see header point 13)
                  1 = REFUSE (state is 'off'; OR state is 'switch'/'coop' and a
                              preflight check failed -- for coop this includes
                              an unresolvable, non-allowlisted, or adjudication
                              role)
                  2 = WAIT   (state is 'auto' and either a preflight check
                              failed, or preflight passed but
                              heimdall-session-usage did not report "crossed"
                              -- print the reasons, do not route blind; `coop`
                              never returns WAIT -- see header point 13)
  coop add/remove/list : add/remove exit 0 on success; add exits 1 (2 if
                  --strict) refusing an adjudication role or an unresolvable
                  argument, never writing the config in that case. list is a
                  never-fail-caller telemetry read, matching status/where.
  arm           : mirrors check's own contract exactly -- 0 = ROUTE, 1 = REFUSE,
                  2 = WAIT -- because arm always ends by running that exact
                  preflight against whatever config is now on disk (the one it
                  just wrote, or the untouched original if it refused to write).
                  A write failure during arm -> 3 if --strict, else 0 (never-
                  fail-caller, matching set's own contract for the same failure
                  mode). coop is deliberately not an arm --state choice (header
                  point 13) -- arm's target_provider/operator_key_env
                  self-provisioning is an orthogonal axis to coop's per-role
                  allowlist; use `set coop` + `coop add` instead.
  argument-parsing errors (any subcommand, including an invalid state to `set`
  or `arm --state`) : 0 unless --strict -> 2
  unexpected internal error (outer safety net; check() fails closed on its
  own and should never actually reach this) : 0 unless --strict -> 3
"""

import argparse
import ipaddress
import json
import os
import sqlite3
import subprocess
import sys
import http.client
import urllib.error
import urllib.parse
import urllib.request

SCHEMA = 1
VALID_STATES = ("off", "auto", "switch", "coop")

DEFAULT_CFG = {
    "schema": SCHEMA,
    "state": "off",
    "operator_key_env": "",
    "endpoint": "http://127.0.0.1:20128",
    "omniroute_db_path": "",
    "cliproxyapi_dir": "",
    "target_provider": "",
    "tos_flagged_providers": [],
    "noauth_providers": [],
    # PATH ONLY, never a secret. The OmniRoute gateway enforces requireLogin on
    # its /v1/* inference surface, so a routed child needs a gateway token in
    # ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN. Storing the PATH here (and the token in a 0600 file
    # the operator minted themselves) keeps this config secret-free -- the same
    # rule operator_key_env follows: hmd records WHERE a credential lives, never
    # the credential. `token-file` refuses a path that is group/world-readable.
    "gateway_token_file": "",
    # The model id hmd pins on a routed child when the operator has not pinned
    # one themselves. EMPTY BY DEFAULT, and an empty value changes nothing: the
    # anthropic_model_pinned check fails exactly as it always did. Set it (or let
    # `arm` resolve it) and `auto` becomes genuinely automatic -- see
    # _model_pin_reject_reason for why an unset ANTHROPIC_MODEL was, until this
    # field existed, an unconditional veto on every unattended launch.
    "fallback_model": "",
    # Explicit, per-role allowlist for state=="coop" -- see header point 13.
    # EMPTY BY DEFAULT: coop with no roles configured routes NOTHING. Only
    # `coop add <role>` (cmd_coop_add) may append to this list, and it refuses
    # outright -- never silently skips -- an adjudication role (reuses
    # bin/lib/hmd-adjudication-set.sh's hmd_is_adjudication, the single source
    # of truth also used by bin/heimdall-precheck-agent and
    # bin/lib/hmd-route-claude, never re-declared here). Sanitized on load to a
    # sorted list of unique, non-empty strings -- see load_config().
    "coop_roles": [],
}

# EMPTY BY DESIGN (owner decision, 2026-08-26). hmd's OWN gate is unchanged --
# target_provider_allowed still refuses any provider named in
# `tos_flagged_providers` (deny = this set | config's additions, see
# run_preflight below) -- only the STARTING set that field adds to has
# changed, from ~30 entries to none. Config may still only ADD to this set,
# never remove or override an entry that IS present: an operator who wants a
# specific provider denied still lists it in `tos_flagged_providers` and it
# is refused exactly as before -- see header point 3.
#
# WHY: the previous ~30-entry set was copied verbatim from
# docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md, Section 2 -- a table
# OmniRoute's own maintainers built to flag providers whose ToS prohibits
# being PROXIED/RESOLD through OmniRoute's free-tier, POOLED-credential
# routing (e.g. groq: "prohibits reselling, sublicensing, or distributing
# API access"; together: "prohibits transferring, distributing, reselling...
# on a s[tandalone basis]"). That is a real conflict for OmniRoute's free
# tier, but this tool never touches OmniRoute's shared credentials at all --
# header point 4 is OPERATOR'S OWN API keys ONLY, a direct pinned request
# through a locally-hosted gateway the operator runs themselves. Copying the
# free-tier table into THIS tool's deny list conflated "OmniRoute's free-tier
# PROXIED access to provider X" with "provider X itself": using the
# operator's own key against, say, Mistral's official first-party API is not
# a ToS conflict with anyone. docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-
# isolation.md S5 independently found this exact shape to be the
# structurally CLEAN one -- its own worked example is a pinned
# `mistral/mistral-large-latest` request, which "never enters the
# multi-provider candidate pool" and (chatCore.ts:2733) has "no other target
# to fail over to," i.e. it cannot cascade into Tier-1 either. With the old
# table covering nearly every real provider name by design (the assessment
# doc flagged ~30 across its "caution" and lower-confidence tiers),
# target_provider_allowed could never pass and the fallback feature was
# permanently inert regardless of an operator's own choice. The operator
# turns fallback on EXPLICITLY (`heimdall-fallback set auto|switch` is a
# deliberate act hmd never performs itself -- see "NO AUTO-SWITCH
# CONTRADICTION" above) and has already accepted the data-handling tradeoff
# (2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md's disqualifier-1 finding; the
# credential-isolation doc's own "OUT OF SCOPE" explicitly declines to
# re-litigate "data-handling/no-train/retention posture of Mistral or LLM7 --
# owner has explicitly accepted that risk"). A built-in list overriding that
# explicit, informed choice is hmd deciding for the operator.
#
# WHAT THIS DOES NOT TOUCH: the Tier-1 boundary -- tier1_credential_absent,
# anthropic_model_pinned, prefer_claude_code_flag_off, and
# no_delegated_sidecar (header point 1a-1d) -- is what actually protects the
# operator's own Anthropic account, and none of those four checks reads this
# set at all. See test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh's dedicated falsifiers
# proving a `claude` provider_connections row is still refused, and
# state=switch still REFUSEs on a failing Tier-1 check, with this set empty.
BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS = frozenset()

# Providers OmniRoute serves via a SYNTHETIC "noauth" connection instead of a
# provider_connections credential row -- sourced verbatim (READ, not guessed)
# from docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md's own
# citation of OmniRoute's NOAUTH_PROVIDERS / no-auth WEB_COOKIE_PROVIDERS
# list. `claude`/`claude-web` are explicitly NOT in it (that doc's own
# emphasis), so this can never collide with the Tier-1 boundary above. An
# operator key is genuinely not required to route to one of these -- that is
# a property of OmniRoute's own architecture, not a gap in this tool. See
# header point 10.
NOAUTH_PROVIDERS = frozenset({
    "devin-cli-agentic", "opencode", "duckduckgo-web", "cloudflare-playground",
    "felo-web", "theoldllm", "chipotle", "veoaifree-web", "auggie", "zcode",
    "codex-app-server", "uncloseai", "aihorde",
})

# Usability ranking inputs for arm's self-pick, so the happy path does not
# default-select a no-auth provider that cannot actually serve a request.
# Every set below is read DIRECTLY off the provider's own entry in OmniRoute's
# src/shared/constants/providers/noauth.ts -- named fields, not inference:
#
#   serviceKinds        -- ["video"] is not a chat surface at all
#   isLocalCli: true    -- OmniRoute shells out to a binary that must be
#                          installed AND separately authenticated on this
#                          machine (e.g. `auggie login`). "No API key stored
#                          by OmniRoute" is not the same as "works keyless".
#   hasFree: false      -- no free tier, so keyless routing has nothing to use
#   toolCalling         -- "emulated" means prompt-injected then text-parsed,
#                          not native tool calling
#
# CORRECTION, recorded deliberately: an earlier revision of this block claimed
# `aihorde` carries an explicit `tools` denial via unsupportedParams. That is
# FALSE and was propagated from a subagent report without being checked.
# `unsupportedParams` is a per-MODEL field resolved through
# open-sse/config/providerRegistry.ts, which contains NO aihorde entry at all,
# so the lookup returns empty and aihorde's tool capability falls through to
# the same optimistic heuristic as everything else. There is no provider-level
# tools denial for any no-auth provider. aihorde is in fact hasFree:true,
# non-localCli, llm -- a legitimate tier-0 candidate.
#
# HONESTY BOUNDARY: `toolCalling` is unset for most entries, and unset means
# OmniRoute's optimistic heuristic, NOT measured support. Several tier-0
# providers are reverse-engineered consumer web UIs where real native tool
# calling is doubtful. Ranking first means "nothing in the registry says it is
# unusable", never "measured capable". Do not render this as a capability
# promise.
NOAUTH_NOT_LLM = frozenset({
    "veoaifree-web",          # serviceKinds: ["video"]
})
NOAUTH_LOCAL_CLI = frozenset({
    "devin-cli-agentic", "auggie", "zcode", "codex-app-server",
})
NOAUTH_NO_FREE_TIER = frozenset({
    "devin-cli-agentic", "auggie", "zcode", "codex-app-server",
})
NOAUTH_TOOLS_EMULATED = frozenset({
    "devin-cli-agentic", "duckduckgo-web",
})


def _arm_provider_rank(provider):
    """Deterministic usability rank for arm's self-pick (lower is better).
    Ties break alphabetically so the pick stays reproducible -- a
    nondeterministic default would make `arm` unauditable."""
    if provider in NOAUTH_NOT_LLM:
        return 3
    if provider in NOAUTH_LOCAL_CLI or provider in NOAUTH_NO_FREE_TIER:
        return 2
    if provider in NOAUTH_TOOLS_EMULATED:
        return 1
    return 0


# One-time, plain explanation surfaced in BOTH `status` and `check` whenever
# the pinned target_provider resolves to a no-auth route (never nagged, never
# repeated beyond the one line) -- the owner's own framing: keyless does not
# mean risk-free, it means OmniRoute's free/no-auth tier, the same weakest-
# contractual-terms category the ToS-conflict flag exists for (see
# docs/analysis/2026-08-23-omniroute-assessment.md and header point 10).
NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT = (
    "target_provider '%s' is served keyless via OmniRoute's own no-auth "
    "connection path -- no operator key is required. Keyless routes are "
    "OmniRoute's free/no-auth tier, the same weakest-contractual-terms "
    "category the ToS-conflict deny list exists for; target_provider_allowed "
    "still applies independently and is not weakened by this."
)

# Best-effort session-usage pre-exhaustion consultation for state=="auto" --
# see header point 11. Four verdicts, all load-bearing: "unknown" must never
# be read as "under", so the note text below is deliberately distinct for
# every one of the four, and _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict() only ever
# returns a value from this exact tuple -- any other outcome it sees
# collapses to "unknown" there, never invents a fifth state here. "crossed"
# is the ONE value _verdict() allow-lists as a necessary (not sufficient)
# condition for ROUTE under auto -- see _verdict() itself.
SESSION_USAGE_VERDICTS = ("unconfigured", "unknown", "under", "crossed")
SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT = {
    "crossed": (
        "pre-exhaustion threshold CROSSED (~95% of session limit) -- the "
        "checks above are still the ONLY thing that decides ROUTE/WAIT/"
        "REFUSE; this authorizes nothing on its own"
    ),
    "unconfigured": "tracking not configured (see heimdall-session-usage)",
    "unknown": "could not be determined -- NOT assumed to be under threshold",
}

# PHASE 5 (2026-09-05) -- a live operator 429 (HTTP 429, session limit,
# resets 2:30pm) landed at the EXACT moment this line printed a flat "under
# the pre-exhaustion threshold" with full confidence: five_hour/seven_day
# were genuinely low, and no OTHER window had ever been observed in the
# harness's own rate_limits payload before now (see bin/heimdall-session-
# usage's own PHASE 5 docstring). A bare "under the pre-exhaustion
# threshold" reads as an unqualified "you are safe" -- the true claim this
# tool can stand behind is narrower: "not crossed among the windows this
# render could see". heimdall-session-usage's PHASE 5 names exactly that
# set as `windows_seen`; "under" is now generated FROM that field (see
# _session_usage_under_note) instead of living as a static dict entry that
# cannot express it -- that is why "under" was pulled out of
# SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT above while the other three verdicts, which never
# claimed a positive "you have capacity" in the first place, stay exactly
# as they were.
def _session_usage_under_note(windows_seen):
    if windows_seen:
        return (
            "under the pre-exhaustion threshold FOR THE WINDOWS THIS CHECK "
            "CAN SEE (%s) -- a limit under a different name is NOT ruled "
            "out and would be invisible here" % ", ".join(windows_seen)
        )
    return (
        "BLIND -- no rate-limit window was observed at all, so this is NOT "
        "confirmation of remaining capacity; a session/weekly/other limit "
        "may already be exhausted without this check ever seeing it"
    )


# Structural check for the "window" field heimdall-session-usage's PHASE 5
# can now emit ("extra:<name>", and "<...>+extra:<name>" combos -- see its
# own module docstring). `<name>` is an arbitrary dict key lifted straight
# from an external rate_limits payload with no sanitization at that layer;
# `window` only ever reaches this file to be interpolated into a printed
# line (never executed, never used as a path), but "only ever printed" is
# not a reason to skip validating untrusted input at a trust boundary
# (header's own "MUST FAIL CLOSED" standard). A window string is accepted
# only if EVERY "+"-separated token is either a fixed PHASE 3/4 literal or
# an "extra:" token with a short, safe charset; anything else fails closed
# to None, same as the fixed allow-list already did below pre-PHASE-5.
_BASE_WINDOW_TOKENS = frozenset({"five_hour", "seven_day", "reactive_429", "all", "both"})


def _session_window_token_ok(tok):
    if tok in _BASE_WINDOW_TOKENS:
        return True
    if tok.startswith("extra:"):
        name = tok[len("extra:"):]
        return 1 <= len(name) <= 64 and all(c.isalnum() or c in "_.-" for c in name)
    return False


def _session_window_is_safe(window):
    if not isinstance(window, str) or not window or len(window) > 256:
        return False
    return all(_session_window_token_ok(tok) for tok in window.split("+"))

# Any of these keys appearing in an on-disk config (any value) is how
# OmniRoute's MITM mode or a blind trust of its advisory ToS flag would be
# spelled. Their mere presence marks the whole config corrupt. See header
# points 2 and 3.
FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS = frozenset({
    "mitm", "mitm_enabled", "tproxy", "tproxy_enabled",
    "root_ca", "decrypt_mode", "trust_omniroute_tos_flag",
})

# An operator_key_env naming a Claude/Anthropic/Claude-Code credential is
# never "the operator's own third-party key" -- see header point 4.
FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS = ("ANTHROPIC", "CLAUDE")

LOCAL_HOSTNAMES = frozenset({"localhost", "127.0.0.1", "::1"})

# Tier-1 subscription providers in OmniRoute's own provider_connections table
# -- docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3's sweep
# list narrowed to the two that are the SAME Anthropic account (Claude Code
# OAuth and a claude.ai session cookie respectively). See header point 1a.
TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS = ("claude", "claude-web")

# upstream_proxy_config.mode values that mean the retry/primary leg for a
# provider is handled by a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or Dario) rather
# than OmniRoute's own native call -- OmniRoute itself can hold zero Tier-1
# tokens while this is still true. See header point 1d, audit S1a path 8 and
# residual risk 2. Sourced verbatim from a live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682)
# install's own src/lib/db/migrations/138_dario_fallback_backend.sql: "mode
# is a free TEXT column already ('native' | 'cliproxyapi' | 'dario' |
# 'fallback')" -- 'native' is the only non-delegating value; 'fallback' means
# the RETRY leg goes to whichever of cliproxyapi/dario `fallback_backend`
# names (default 'cliproxyapi'), so it is delegating regardless of which
# backend it names. This check ignores the row's own `enabled` flag on
# purpose -- fail-closed treats a configured-but-disabled delegation the same
# as an active one, since re-enabling it is a single flag flip this tool does
# not control or continuously observe. NOTE this is the table/column the
# check ACTUALLY queries -- provider_connections (queried above for Tier-1)
# has no 'mode' column on a real install at all (confirmed: 46 real columns,
# zero named 'mode'); an earlier version of this check queried it there by
# mistake, inferred from the audit's prose rather than the real schema, and
# always passed vacuously.
SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES = frozenset({"cliproxyapi", "dario", "fallback"})

# Values that count as "the widening flag is off" for
# OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS -- unset and
# every common falsy spelling. Anything else (including "1", "true", "yes")
# fails the check. See header point 1c.
_ENV_FLAG_OFF_VALUES = frozenset({"", "0", "false", "no", "off"})


class Rejected(Exception):
    """Raised by _Parser.error() instead of argparse's default sys.exit(2)."""


class _Parser(argparse.ArgumentParser):
    def error(self, message):
        raise Rejected(message)


def _cfg_path(repo):
    return os.path.join(repo, ".heimdall", "fallback.json")


def load_config(repo):
    """Fail-closed config load. Absent, unreadable, non-dict, or containing a
    FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS entry all resolve to a fresh DEFAULT_CFG copy
    (state off) -- never a partially-trusted merge. See header point 6. A
    persisted state of 'on' (removed; see header point 8) migrates to 'off',
    the same landing every other invalid/corrupt state value already gets
    below -- never 'auto' or 'switch', both of which are live states an
    operator now has to choose deliberately. This is NOT a lossless landing:
    'on', as its one real caller (bin/lib/issue_loop.py) ever actually
    exercised it -- never passing a tier argument -- ROUTEd whenever
    preflight passed, exactly like 'switch' still does today. 'off' is not
    that same outcome: off's own "state" check in run_preflight() fails
    unconditionally whenever state=='off', so a migrated config can never
    ROUTE again until the operator explicitly re-arms to 'auto' or 'switch'
    -- 'on's tier-blind ROUTE path is genuinely gone. That loss is
    deliberate: 'off' is the one landing guaranteed to never silently hand
    out MORE routing than the operator had, at the cost of not preserving
    what they did have. The OTHER capability 'on' offered -- refusing
    non-low-tier work outright regardless of preflight -- had zero live
    callers to begin with, so that half really does cost nobody anything.
    'auto' is not a safer substitute for 'off' here either -- it is a
    differently-natured, exhaustion-reactive gate (header point 11) that
    would silently substitute session-usage judgment for the tier judgment
    'on' actually made, and it can WAIT indefinitely rather than give a
    clear answer when usage cannot be confirmed crossed, a worse landing
    than a config that now resolves cleanly (REFUSE) every time. Returns
    (cfg, corrupt_reason); corrupt_reason is None when the on-disk config
    (or its absence) was clean.
    """
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except FileNotFoundError:
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), None
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config file is unreadable or not valid JSON"
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config is not a JSON object"
    forbidden_present = sorted(k for k in raw if k in FORBIDDEN_CONFIG_KEYS)
    if forbidden_present:
        return dict(DEFAULT_CFG), "config contains forbidden key(s): " + ", ".join(forbidden_present)

    cfg = dict(DEFAULT_CFG)
    for key in DEFAULT_CFG:
        if key in raw:
            cfg[key] = raw[key]
    state_corrupt_reason = None
    raw_state = cfg.get("state")
    if raw_state == "on":
        # 'on' was removed (owner directive: only off/auto/switch survive).
        # Migrate to 'off' -- the same uniform landing every other invalid
        # state value already gets a few lines below -- rather than guessing
        # a replacement state on the operator's behalf; see the docstring
        # above and header point 8.
        cfg["state"] = "off"
        state_corrupt_reason = (
            "state 'on' no longer exists (removed; valid states are now "
            "off/auto/switch) -- migrated to 'off' rather than picking a "
            "replacement for you: 'off' is a hard, unconditional refuse "
            "(see run_preflight's own state check), so 'on's prior "
            "tier-blind ROUTE is genuinely gone here, not preserved -- "
            "deliberate, since 'off' is the one landing guaranteed to "
            "never hand out MORE routing than you had; run "
            "'heimdall-fallback set auto' or 'set switch' to re-arm"
        )
    elif raw_state not in VALID_STATES:
        cfg["state"] = "off"
        state_corrupt_reason = "state %r is not one of off/auto/switch/coop -- reset to off" % (raw_state,)
    if isinstance(cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers"), list):
        cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] = [p for p in cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] if isinstance(p, str)]
    else:
        cfg["tos_flagged_providers"] = []
    if isinstance(cfg.get("noauth_providers"), list):
        cfg["noauth_providers"] = [p for p in cfg["noauth_providers"] if isinstance(p, str)]
    else:
        cfg["noauth_providers"] = []
    if isinstance(cfg.get("coop_roles"), list):
        cfg["coop_roles"] = sorted({
            p.strip() for p in cfg["coop_roles"] if isinstance(p, str) and p.strip()
        })
    else:
        cfg["coop_roles"] = []
    for key in ("operator_key_env", "endpoint", "target_provider", "omniroute_db_path", "cliproxyapi_dir"):
        if not isinstance(cfg.get(key), str):
            cfg[key] = DEFAULT_CFG[key]
    return cfg, state_corrupt_reason


def save_config(repo, cfg):
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    os.makedirs(os.path.dirname(path), exist_ok=True)
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        json.dump(cfg, fh, indent=2, sort_keys=True)
        fh.write("\n")
    os.replace(tmp, path)


def _is_local_endpoint(endpoint):
    try:
        parsed = urllib.parse.urlparse(endpoint)
    except ValueError:
        return False
    host = parsed.hostname
    if not host:
        return False
    if host.lower() in LOCAL_HOSTNAMES:
        return True
    try:
        return ipaddress.ip_address(host).is_loopback
    except ValueError:
        return False


class _NoRedirect(urllib.request.HTTPRedirectHandler):
    """Never follow a redirect while probing liveness.

    A 3xx already proves the server is answering HTTP, and following it
    could bounce the probe onto a slow or off-box URL -- which would turn a
    bounded local check into an unbounded one."""

    def redirect_request(self, req, fp, code, msg, headers, newurl):
        return None


def _probe_timeout():
    """Seconds to wait for response HEADERS. Deliberately tight.

    This is a routing gate, not a health dashboard: a gateway that cannot
    answer a loopback request within this budget is not usable as a
    fallback, so timing out and refusing to route is the CORRECT verdict,
    not a false alarm."""
    raw = os.environ.get("HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_PROBE_TIMEOUT") or ""
    try:
        val = float(raw)
    except ValueError:
        return 3.0
    return val if val > 0 else 3.0


def _endpoint_reachable(endpoint):
    """Real HTTP liveness probe -- stdlib only.

    A TCP connect is NOT sufficient and used to be what this did. A wedged
    server -- or a webpack dev server still compiling the route -- completes
    the TCP handshake while never answering a single HTTP request. That is
    exactly the state that made this check report [OK] while every real
    call to the gateway timed out. So: send an actual request and require
    response HEADERS back.

    ANY HTTP status counts as alive. 307/401/404 all prove something is
    serving HTTP here; this answers "is anything answering", never "is this
    request authorized" -- so the probe is sent UNAUTHENTICATED, which also
    means the gateway token is never transmitted by a liveness check.

    Fails CLOSED: timeout, refusal, or malformed reply -> False.

    Overridable ONLY for hermetic tests via
    HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_ASSUME_REACHABLE (nothing outside
    test/heimdall-fallback.test.sh sets this in this repo)."""
    override = os.environ.get("HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_ASSUME_REACHABLE")
    if override is not None:
        return override == "1"
    try:
        parsed = urllib.parse.urlparse(endpoint)
        if not parsed.hostname:
            return False
    except ValueError:
        return False
    req = urllib.request.Request(endpoint, method="GET")
    req.add_header("User-Agent", "heimdall-fallback-liveness/1")
    # ProxyHandler({}) DISABLES proxying outright. build_opener() would
    # otherwise install a default ProxyHandler that honours http_proxy /
    # HTTPS_PROXY from the environment, so an ambient proxy var would send
    # this probe through a third party -- and a dead or unrelated proxy
    # would report a perfectly healthy loopback gateway as unreachable.
    # The endpoint here is loopback by construction; it must never leave
    # the machine. The old TCP-connect probe could not be diverted this
    # way, so this is a hazard the HTTP rewrite introduced and must close.
    opener = urllib.request.build_opener(urllib.request.ProxyHandler({}), _NoRedirect)
    try:
        with opener.open(req, timeout=_probe_timeout()):
            return True
    except urllib.error.HTTPError:
        # 4xx/5xx/3xx: the server answered HTTP. That is liveness.
        return True
    except (urllib.error.URLError, http.client.HTTPException, OSError):
        return False


def _resolve_omniroute_db_path(cfg):
    """Where to find OmniRoute's own SQLite DB for the credential-absence
    check (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3).
    Priority: explicit cfg field -> DATA_DIR env var (OmniRoute's own
    override, src/lib/dataPaths.ts) -> the documented default ~/.omniroute.
    XDG_CONFIG_HOME is a second documented override but its exact path-join
    semantics are not confirmed in the audit, so it is deliberately not
    replicated here -- an unresolved limitation, not a silent guess."""
    configured = cfg.get("omniroute_db_path") or ""
    if configured:
        return os.path.expanduser(configured)
    data_dir = os.environ.get("DATA_DIR") or ""
    if data_dir:
        return os.path.join(os.path.expanduser(data_dir), "storage.sqlite")
    return os.path.expanduser(os.path.join("~", ".omniroute", "storage.sqlite"))


def _resolve_cliproxyapi_dir(cfg):
    """Where CLIProxyAPI's own config directory would live if installed --
    the one real filesystem->Claude-credential route (audit S1a, path 8).
    Priority mirrors CLIProxyAPI's own resolution order exactly: cfg field ->
    CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR env var -> ~/.cli-proxy-api default (quoted
    verbatim from OmniRoute's own cliProxyConfigDir())."""
    configured = cfg.get("cliproxyapi_dir") or ""
    if configured:
        return os.path.expanduser(configured)
    env_dir = os.environ.get("CLIPROXYAPI_CONFIG_DIR") or ""
    if env_dir:
        return os.path.expanduser(env_dir)
    return os.path.expanduser(os.path.join("~", ".cli-proxy-api"))


def _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg):
    """The built-in NOAUTH_PROVIDERS set plus any operator ADDITIONS from
    config's `noauth_providers` -- same additions-only shape as
    tos_flagged_providers (config can never remove a built-in entry, only
    name one more). An addition is the operator's own factual declaration
    ("I know this specific provider is keyless") for a provider this tool's
    hardcoded, sourced list has not caught up with yet -- never a guess this
    tool makes on its own. See header point 10."""
    return NOAUTH_PROVIDERS | {
        p.strip().lower() for p in cfg.get("noauth_providers", []) if isinstance(p, str)
    }


def _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg):
    """Whether cfg's pinned target_provider is a no-auth route -- see
    _resolve_noauth_providers and header point 10. An empty target_provider
    is never no-auth (nothing is pinned to check)."""
    provider = (cfg.get("target_provider") or "").strip().lower()
    return bool(provider) and provider in _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg)


def _open_omniroute_db_ro(path):
    """Open OmniRoute's SQLite DB strictly read-only via a URI connection --
    this tool must never create or mutate the operator's real database, not
    even by the accidental create-if-missing default of sqlite3.connect()."""
    uri = "file:%s?mode=ro" % urllib.parse.quote(path)
    return sqlite3.connect(uri, uri=True, timeout=1.0)


def _query_omniroute(path, sql, params=()):
    """Run one read-only query against the OmniRoute DB. Returns (rows,
    error) -- error is None on success, and rows is None whenever error is
    set. A failed connect (e.g. not a SQLite file at all) and a failed
    execute (e.g. a missing table/column) collapse to this same shape --
    callers must always fail closed on error, never treat None rows as an
    empty result."""
    try:
        conn = _open_omniroute_db_ro(path)
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        return None, str(exc)
    try:
        rows = conn.execute(sql, params).fetchall()
        return rows, None
    except sqlite3.Error as exc:
        return None, str(exc)
    finally:
        conn.close()


def _blocked_providers_note(repo):
    """blockedProviders is an OmniRoute DASHBOARD setting that hides a
    provider from /v1/models -- it is NEVER consulted by
    getProviderCredentials() and never stops routing (docs/analysis/2026-08-
    25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3, S6). If an operator has placed a
    same-named key in THIS tool's own config -- most plausibly believing it
    acts as a kill switch here -- load_config()'s allowlist copy already
    drops it silently (it is not a DEFAULT_CFG key); this returns a warning
    string so that silence is never mistaken for the field having done
    something. See header point 7."""
    path = _cfg_path(repo)
    try:
        with open(path, "r", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            raw = json.load(fh)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return None
    if not isinstance(raw, dict):
        return None
    for key in ("blockedProviders", "blocked_providers"):
        if key in raw:
            return (
                "config key '%s' is set but is never evidence of anything here -- it "
                "only hides a provider from OmniRoute's /v1/models listing and is not "
                "consulted by credential resolution; it has been ignored" % key
            )
    return None


def _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict(repo):
    """Best-effort consult of bin/heimdall-session-usage's own pre-exhaustion
    verdict (~95% of session limit) -- see header point 11. Feeds TWO things
    for state=="auto": the [INFO] line, AND _verdict()'s own ROUTE-vs-WAIT
    decision, where "crossed" is a necessary (not sufficient) condition for
    ROUTE. It does NOT feed anything for off/on/switch -- those states never
    call this function at all (see cmd_check).

    Returns a (verdict, window, windows_seen) TUPLE. `window` (2026-08-29,
    heimdall-session-usage PHASE 3; extended 2026-08-30 PHASE 4; extended
    2026-09-05 PHASE 5 to also allow an "extra:<name>" token -- see
    _session_window_is_safe) names WHICH observation(s) crossed, or None.
    `windows_seen` (2026-09-05, heimdall-session-usage PHASE 5) names every
    window the harness actually exposed this render (e.g. ["five_hour",
    "seven_day"]), independent of whether any of them crossed -- or None
    when heimdall-session-usage predates PHASE 5, or never had a real
    snapshot to name windows from. Both are METADATA ONLY: _verdict()'s
    ROUTE/WAIT/REFUSE decision reacts to `verdict` alone, exactly as before.
    `window` is surfaced in cmd_check's [INFO] line so an operator can see
    which limit actually crossed; `windows_seen` is surfaced in the "under"
    note text itself (_session_usage_under_note) so an "under" reading is
    never mistaken for an unqualified "you are safe" (PHASE 5's whole reason
    for existing -- see the note above SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT). A
    missing/unrecognized value for either degrades to None -- the same
    fail-open standard `verdict` itself already holds to below.

    Every failure mode (missing binary, non-executable, timeout, non-JSON
    stdout, a JSON body with no recognized "verdict") is swallowed HERE,
    locally, and collapses to the tool's own pre-existing "unknown" bucket --
    never a fifth state, and never left to escape to cmd_check's outer
    fail-closed net, which would incorrectly turn an already-computed
    ROUTE/WAIT into a REFUSE. This consultation must never change any
    *individual preflight check's* own ok/detail -- each of those is computed
    independently in run_preflight() and is untouched by this function.
    _verdict()'s overall ROUTE/WAIT decision under auto is a separate,
    additional gate this function's return value DOES feed -- that is the
    entire point of it existing (header point 11), not a side effect to
    guard against. heimdall-session-usage's own `status` exits 1 for a
    genuine CROSSED verdict -- that is not an error, so success is judged by
    whether stdout parses as JSON with a recognized "verdict", never by the
    return code.
    """
    exe = os.environ.get("HEIMDALL_FALLBACK_SESSION_USAGE_BIN") or os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "heimdall-session-usage"
    )
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            [exe, "--repo", repo, "status", "--json"],
            capture_output=True, text=True, timeout=3,
        )
    except Exception:
        return "unknown", None, None
    try:
        payload = json.loads(proc.stdout)
    except (ValueError, TypeError):
        return "unknown", None, None
    verdict = payload.get("verdict") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if verdict not in SESSION_USAGE_VERDICTS:
        return "unknown", None, None
    window = payload.get("window") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if not _session_window_is_safe(window):
        window = None
    windows_seen = payload.get("windows_seen") if isinstance(payload, dict) else None
    if not (
        isinstance(windows_seen, list)
        and 1 <= len(windows_seen) <= 32
        and all(isinstance(w, str) and 1 <= len(w) <= 64 for w in windows_seen)
    ):
        windows_seen = None
    return verdict, window, windows_seen


TIER1_MODEL_PREFIXES = ("claude", "claude-web")


def _model_pin_reject_reason(model, origin):
    """-> a reason string if `model` is unsafe to route with, else None.

    `origin` names where the id came from, so the message tells the operator
    which knob to fix.

    Two rejections, and the second one closes a real hole this check had. The
    documented intent is that a bare `claude-*` id must never reach OmniRoute,
    because OmniRoute has an explicit branch matching it to provider 'claude'.
    The original test for that was `"/" not in model` -- which accepts
    `claude/sonnet` and `claude-web/anything`, i.e. the provider prefix that
    names the Tier-1 subscription EXPLICITLY sails through the check that
    exists to keep Tier-1 out. tier1_credential_absent is the primary defense
    and would still hold, but a second line of defense that admits the exact
    id it is defending against is not a second line of defense.

    Only the provider SEGMENT is matched, never a substring: an id like
    `anthropic/claude-3-5-sonnet` names provider `anthropic` (a paid API key,
    not the operator's subscription) and is legitimate. Matching 'claude'
    anywhere in the string would reject it, which is why the split is on the
    first '/' and compared exactly.
    """
    model = (model or "").strip()
    if not model:
        return None  # caller decides what "absent" means; not this function's job
    if "/" not in model:
        return ("%s='%s' has no explicit provider/ prefix -- an unprefixed id is exactly "
                "the bare form OmniRoute's routing branch matches" % (origin, model))
    provider_segment = model.split("/", 1)[0].strip().lower()
    if provider_segment in TIER1_MODEL_PREFIXES:
        return ("%s='%s' names Tier-1 provider '%s' explicitly -- routing fallback traffic "
                "at the operator's OWN Claude subscription is the exact boundary this tool "
                "exists to hold" % (origin, model, provider_segment))
    return None


def _adjudication_lib_path():
    """Path to bin/lib/hmd-adjudication-set.sh, the single source of truth
    for judgment-role classification (hmd_is_adjudication()). Resolved
    relative to THIS file's own location -- same pattern
    _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict already uses to find its sibling
    heimdall-session-usage -- so it is correct regardless of --repo or any
    sandboxed HEIMDALL_HOME a caller (or a hermetic test) sets."""
    return os.path.join(
        os.path.dirname(os.path.abspath(__file__)), "lib", "hmd-adjudication-set.sh"
    )


def _is_adjudication_role(role):
    """True iff `role` is a judgment role per hmd_is_adjudication() (bin/lib/
    hmd-adjudication-set.sh) -- reviewer, verifier, security-auditor,
    incident-responder, or the *-review*/*-audit*/*-verif* glob. Shelled
    out to, never re-declared: two independent copies of "what counts as
    judgment" is how a coop allowlist quietly drifts from the adjudication
    fence that is supposed to be its hard backstop (header point 13).

    Fails CLOSED on any plumbing failure (library missing, bash missing, an
    unexpected non-zero exit) by treating an unclassifiable role AS
    adjudication -- i.e. barred from coop. This is the opposite fail
    direction from most plumbing checks in this file (which fail closed on
    ROUTING but open on unrelated plumbing errors), because the unsafe
    outcome here is the same either way: a role that cannot be PROVEN safe
    must never be admitted to a standing OmniRoute allowlist on the
    strength of "the classifier was unavailable".
    """
    role = (role or "").strip()
    if not role:
        return False  # empty/absent is never a positive match -- nothing to bar
    lib = _adjudication_lib_path()
    if not os.path.isfile(lib):
        return True  # fail closed: cannot prove NOT adjudication
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["bash", "-c", '. "$1" && hmd_is_adjudication "$2"', "_", lib, role],
            capture_output=True, timeout=3,
        )
    except Exception:
        return True  # fail closed: classifier unavailable
    return proc.returncode == 0


def _coop_role_allowed(cfg, role):
    """Whether `role` may route under state=="coop". Returns (allowed, reason).

    Three gates, in order -- see header point 13:
      1. role must be non-empty -- a bare, unidentified `check` can never
         silently ROUTE under coop; there is nothing to consult the
         allowlist against.
      2. role must never be a judgment role, REGARDLESS of the on-disk
         allowlist -- re-enforced HERE, not just at `coop add` config time,
         because a config file can be hand-edited, or predate a later
         hmd_is_adjudication() classification change. check-time is the
         last line of defense and must never trust the write-time gate
         alone.
      3. role must be on cfg["coop_roles"] -- default empty, so coop with
         no roles configured routes NOTHING.
    """
    if not role:
        return False, ("no role identified for this check -- coop requires --role (or "
                        "HMD_AGENT_TYPE) to know which allowlist entry to consult; a bare, "
                        "unidentified check can never silently ROUTE under coop")
    if _is_adjudication_role(role):
        return False, ("role '%s' is a judgment/adjudication role -- coop can never route "
                        "judgment traffic, regardless of the on-disk allowlist (see "
                        "bin/lib/hmd-adjudication-set.sh)" % role)
    roles = cfg.get("coop_roles") or []
    if role not in roles:
        return False, ("role '%s' is not on the coop allowlist (coop_roles=%s) -- coop "
                        "routes ONLY explicitly allowlisted roles; use 'heimdall-fallback "
                        "coop add %s' to add it" % (role, roles, role))
    return True, ""


def run_preflight(cfg, role=None):
    """Every gated check runs and reports independently, in a fixed order --
    a failure at 3am names exactly what to fix, never just "preflight
    failed". Order mirrors the header's numbered safety boundary. Returns a
    list of (name, ok, reason) triples; reason is "" when ok is True.

    `role` is optional and consulted ONLY for the trailing coop_role_allowed
    check, appended when cfg["state"] == "coop" (header point 13) -- every
    check above it runs identically regardless of role, exactly as it
    always has for off/auto/switch. Passing role=None (or omitting it)
    under coop is exactly the bare, unidentified `check` case and must FAIL
    that check, never skip it.
    """
    checks = []

    state = cfg.get("state")
    checks.append((
        "state",
        state != "off",
        "" if state != "off" else "fallback state is 'off' (heimdall-fallback set auto|switch|coop)",
    ))

    key_env = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
    target_provider_raw = cfg.get("target_provider") or ""
    if key_env and any(marker in key_env.upper() for marker in FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS):
        # Absolute regardless of no-auth status -- see header point 4/10: the
        # Claude Code OAuth session may never be reused as a fallback key,
        # even for a provider that would otherwise need no key at all.
        checks.append((
            "operator_key", False,
            "operator_key_env '%s' names a Claude/Anthropic credential -- the Claude Code "
            "OAuth session may never be reused as a fallback key" % key_env,
        ))
    elif _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg):
        checks.append(("operator_key", True, NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT % target_provider_raw))
    elif not key_env:
        checks.append(("operator_key", False, "no operator_key_env configured -- an operator-owned key is required"))
    elif not os.environ.get(key_env):
        checks.append(("operator_key", False, "env var '%s' (operator_key_env) is not set" % key_env))
    else:
        checks.append(("operator_key", True, ""))

    endpoint = cfg.get("endpoint") or ""
    local_ok = bool(endpoint) and _is_local_endpoint(endpoint)
    checks.append((
        "endpoint_local", local_ok,
        "" if local_ok else "endpoint '%s' is not a loopback address -- fallback only ever "
                             "targets a locally-hosted gateway" % (endpoint or "(empty)"),
    ))

    if local_ok:
        reachable_ok = _endpoint_reachable(endpoint)
        reachable_reason = "" if reachable_ok else "endpoint '%s' is not reachable (is the local gateway running?)" % endpoint
    else:
        reachable_ok = False
        reachable_reason = "skipped -- endpoint is not local (see endpoint_local)"
    checks.append(("endpoint_reachable", reachable_ok, reachable_reason))

    # -- Tier-1 credential-absence + delegated-sidecar-in-DB share one DB open
    # (docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3/S6; see
    # header point 1a/1d). Absent or unreadable DB is a FAIL for BOTH, never
    # a silent pass -- see header point 1.
    db_path = _resolve_omniroute_db_path(cfg)
    if not os.path.exists(db_path):
        tier1_ok = False
        tier1_reason = (
            "OmniRoute DB not found at '%s' -- absence is not proof of an empty "
            "table, so this refuses rather than passing an unverifiable check "
            "(docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S3)" % db_path
        )
        sidecar_db_ok = False
        sidecar_db_reason = (
            "OmniRoute DB not found at '%s' -- cannot query upstream_proxy_config for a "
            "delegated-sidecar row, so a delegated sidecar cannot be ruled out" % db_path
        )
    else:
        provider_rows, provider_err = _query_omniroute(
            db_path,
            "SELECT DISTINCT provider FROM provider_connections WHERE provider IN (?, ?)",
            TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS,
        )
        if provider_err is not None:
            tier1_ok = False
            tier1_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB at '%s' is unreadable or malformed (%s) -- an unreadable "
                "DB can never positively verify credential absence" % (db_path, provider_err)
            )
        elif provider_rows:
            found = ", ".join(sorted(row[0] for row in provider_rows))
            tier1_ok = False
            tier1_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB has an active Tier-1 provider_connections row (%s) -- "
                "Tier-1 repurposes that credential as a routed backend" % found
            )
        else:
            tier1_ok = True
            tier1_reason = ""

        # upstream_proxy_config, NOT provider_connections -- confirmed against
        # a live OmniRoute 3.8.51 (d82b682) install: provider_connections has
        # 46 real columns and none named 'mode'. mode/fallback_backend live on
        # upstream_proxy_config (src/lib/db/migrations/138_dario_fallback_
        # backend.sql). See SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES for the mode vocabulary.
        #
        # The table's mere ABSENCE is deliberately NOT the same failure mode
        # as "this DB could not be read". test/issue-loop-claude-fix-
        # fallback.test.sh's case (c) -- a real, existing caller's own
        # hermetic fixture, which this tool does not own and must not edit to
        # fit -- carries a provider_connections-only DB that legitimately
        # predates migration 138_dario_fallback_backend.sql. A missing table
        # is a structural fact about a DB's schema VERSION, not an unknown,
        # whenever it is accompanied by independent positive evidence that
        # this really is a readable, on-topic OmniRoute DB: the
        # tier1_credential_absent query against provider_connections, just
        # above, having itself succeeded (provider_err is None) is exactly
        # that evidence. Only when that positive evidence is ALSO missing
        # (provider_connections itself unreadable, so this DB cannot even be
        # confirmed as a genuine OmniRoute DB) does an absent
        # upstream_proxy_config fall back to failing closed on genuine
        # uncertainty, exactly as every other branch here does.
        table_rows, table_err = _query_omniroute(
            db_path,
            "SELECT 1 FROM sqlite_master WHERE type='table' AND name='upstream_proxy_config'",
        )
        if table_err is not None:
            sidecar_db_ok = False
            sidecar_db_reason = (
                "OmniRoute DB at '%s' could not be queried for upstream_proxy_config's "
                "existence (%s) -- cannot positively rule out a delegated sidecar" % (db_path, table_err)
            )
        elif not table_rows:
            if provider_err is None:
                sidecar_db_ok = True
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "upstream_proxy_config does not exist in this OmniRoute DB (schema "
                    "predates migration 138_dario_fallback_backend.sql) -- "
                    "provider_connections is present and queryable, confirming this is a "
                    "genuine (if older) OmniRoute DB whose schema has no delegated-sidecar "
                    "concept at all, so none can exist"
                )
            else:
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB at '%s' has no upstream_proxy_config table AND "
                    "provider_connections is also unreadable (%s) -- cannot confirm this "
                    "is a genuine OmniRoute DB, so a delegated sidecar cannot be ruled "
                    "out" % (db_path, provider_err)
                )
        else:
            proxy_rows, proxy_err = _query_omniroute(
                db_path,
                "SELECT provider_id, mode, fallback_backend FROM upstream_proxy_config "
                "WHERE mode IN (%s)" % ", ".join("?" for _ in SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES),
                tuple(SIDECAR_DELEGATING_MODES),
            )
            if proxy_err is not None:
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB at '%s' could not be queried for a delegated-sidecar "
                    "connection in upstream_proxy_config (%s) -- cannot positively rule "
                    "one out" % (db_path, proxy_err)
                )
            elif proxy_rows:
                found = ", ".join(
                    "%s (mode=%s%s)" % (
                        pid, m, (", fallback_backend=%s" % fb) if m == "fallback" else "",
                    )
                    for pid, m, fb in proxy_rows
                )
                sidecar_db_ok = False
                sidecar_db_reason = (
                    "OmniRoute DB has %d upstream_proxy_config row(s) delegating to an "
                    "embedded proxy (%s) -- a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or Dario) can "
                    "hold a Claude credential independently of OmniRoute's own token "
                    "storage" % (len(proxy_rows), found)
                )
            else:
                sidecar_db_ok = True
                sidecar_db_reason = ""

    checks.append(("tier1_credential_absent", tier1_ok, tier1_reason))

    # An id the OPERATOR pinned outranks a configured one: their explicit choice is
    # never silently overridden, and if it is unsafe they get told so rather than
    # having hmd quietly substitute its own.
    anthropic_model = (os.environ.get("ANTHROPIC_MODEL") or "").strip()
    configured_model = (cfg.get("fallback_model") or "").strip()
    if anthropic_model:
        reason = _model_pin_reject_reason(anthropic_model, "ANTHROPIC_MODEL")
        checks.append(("anthropic_model_pinned", reason is None, reason or ""))
    elif configured_model:
        # ANTHROPIC_MODEL is unset, but hmd will export fallback_model on the routed
        # child before exec (see the FALLBACK PRECEDENCE block in bin/heimdall-route),
        # so the child never emits a bare claude-* id. The safety property the check
        # defends is satisfied by hmd doing the pinning -- so this PASSES, and it is
        # the whole reason `state=auto` can now fire unattended.
        reason = _model_pin_reject_reason(configured_model, "fallback_model")
        if reason is None:
            checks.append((
                "anthropic_model_pinned", True,
                "ANTHROPIC_MODEL is unset, but fallback_model='%s' is configured and hmd "
                "pins it on the routed child before exec" % configured_model,
            ))
        else:
            checks.append(("anthropic_model_pinned", False, reason))
    else:
        checks.append((
            "anthropic_model_pinned", False,
            "ANTHROPIC_MODEL is not set and no fallback_model is configured -- Claude Code "
            "emits bare claude-* model IDs by default, which OmniRoute has an explicit "
            "branch to route to provider 'claude' if a connection exists "
            "(docs/analysis/2026-08-25-omniroute-credential-isolation.md S5). Set "
            "fallback_model (or run `arm`) to let hmd pin one for you",
        ))

    prefer_flag = os.environ.get("OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS") or ""
    prefer_off = prefer_flag.strip().lower() in _ENV_FLAG_OFF_VALUES
    checks.append((
        "prefer_claude_code_flag_off", prefer_off,
        "" if prefer_off else
        "OMNIROUTE_PREFER_CLAUDE_CODE_FOR_UNPREFIXED_CLAUDE_MODELS='%s' -- this is a Tier-1 "
        "WIDENING knob (opposite polarity from what an operator would expect), default "
        "false, must stay off" % prefer_flag,
    ))

    sidecar_dir = _resolve_cliproxyapi_dir(cfg)
    if os.path.exists(sidecar_dir):
        checks.append((
            "no_delegated_sidecar", False,
            "CLIProxyAPI directory exists at '%s' -- a delegated sidecar (CLIProxyAPI or "
            "Dario, OmniRoute's own 'Claude-subscription proxy') can hold a Claude "
            "credential independently of OmniRoute's own DB state" % sidecar_dir,
        ))
    else:
        checks.append(("no_delegated_sidecar", sidecar_db_ok, sidecar_db_reason))

    provider = (cfg.get("target_provider") or "").strip().lower()
    deny = BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS | {p.strip().lower() for p in cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers", [])}
    if not provider:
        checks.append(("target_provider_allowed", False, "no target_provider configured"))
    elif provider in deny:
        checks.append((
            "target_provider_allowed", False,
            "target_provider '%s' is on the ToS-conflict deny list -- hmd never routes to "
            "it regardless of OmniRoute's own advisory flag" % provider,
        ))
    else:
        checks.append(("target_provider_allowed", True, ""))

    if cfg.get("state") == "coop":
        coop_ok, coop_reason = _coop_role_allowed(cfg, role)
        checks.append(("coop_role_allowed", coop_ok, coop_reason))

    return checks


def _verdict(state, checks, session_verdict=None):
    """state decides ROUTE vs REFUSE vs WAIT. `switch` never WAITs: it is an
    explicit always-try state, so a failing preflight is a REFUSE, never a
    silent wait (header point 9). run_preflight's checks always run in full
    regardless of state -- nothing here is ever a short-circuit (header
    point 1).

    `auto` is the one state that is actually exhaustion-gated (header point
    11): a passing preflight is NECESSARY but no longer SUFFICIENT for ROUTE
    under auto -- it additionally requires session_verdict == "crossed".
    Both conditions are necessary; neither is sufficient alone: a failing
    preflight WAITs regardless of session_verdict (95% crossed authorizes
    nothing on its own), and a passing preflight with session_verdict in
    {"under", "unconfigured", "unknown", None} WAITs instead of routing
    (capacity remains, or cannot be confirmed low, so there is nothing to
    react to yet). off/switch never read session_verdict at all -- callers
    pass None for them and this function never inspects it outside the
    `state == "auto"` branch below.
    """
    preflight_ok = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
    if state == "auto":
        if not preflight_ok:
            return "WAIT", 2
        if session_verdict == "crossed":
            return "ROUTE", 0
        if session_verdict == "unknown":
            # Capacity could not be measured at all (tool missing, timed out,
            # or unparseable -- see _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict). Waiting
            # risks a delayed fallback if the measurement stays broken;
            # routing risks burning fallback-provider capacity, and a cold or
            # degraded cache (header point 11's headroom-measurement
            # citation), while Anthropic quota may still have been available.
            # This file fails closed everywhere else (see "MUST FAIL CLOSED"
            # below; tier1_credential_absent and no_delegated_sidecar make
            # the identical "cannot verify -> cannot proceed" call), so it
            # fails closed here too: WAIT, never ROUTE, on an unmeasurable
            # signal. This is its own branch, not a fallthrough into
            # "under" -- see header point 11 for why the two are kept
            # separately reasoned even though both WAIT.
            return "WAIT", 2
        # "under" (capacity confirmed NOT low) or "unconfigured" (tracking
        # never set up): neither is evidence of anything to react to.
        return "WAIT", 2
    if preflight_ok:
        return "ROUTE", 0
    return "REFUSE", 1


def cmd_status(args):
    config_path = _cfg_path(args.repo)
    cfg, corrupt_reason = load_config(args.repo)
    checks = run_preflight(cfg)
    would_pass = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
    key_env = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
    key_present = bool(key_env) and bool(os.environ.get(key_env))
    is_switch = cfg["state"] == "switch"
    switch_warning = (
        "FULL SWITCH: every task, every tier, routes to OmniRoute when preflight passes -- "
        "capability tier is never consulted and there is no no-train guarantee on this path"
        if is_switch else None
    )
    target_provider = cfg.get("target_provider") or ""
    noauth_note = NOAUTH_ROUTE_NOTE_FMT % target_provider if _target_provider_is_noauth(cfg) else None
    if args.json:
        payload = {
            "state": cfg["state"],
            "config_path": config_path,
            "full_switch": is_switch,
            "would_preflight_pass": would_pass,
            "operator_key_env": key_env,
            "operator_key_configured": key_present,
            "endpoint": cfg.get("endpoint", ""),
            "target_provider": target_provider,
            "corrupt": corrupt_reason,
            "switch_warning": switch_warning,
            "noauth_route": noauth_note,
        }
        print(json.dumps(payload, sort_keys=True))
    else:
        print("heimdall-fallback status")
        print("  config:                %s" % config_path)
        state_line = cfg["state"]
        if is_switch:
            state_line += "   *** %s ***" % switch_warning
        print("  state:                 %s" % state_line)
        print("  would preflight pass:  %s" % ("yes" if would_pass else "no"))
        print("  operator key:          %s (%s)" % (key_env or "(unset)", "configured" if key_present else "absent"))
        print("  endpoint:              %s" % (cfg.get("endpoint") or "(unset)"))
        print("  target provider:       %s" % (target_provider or "(unset)"))
        if corrupt_reason:
            print("  NOTE: on-disk config was corrupt (%s) -- reset to safe defaults (state=off)" % corrupt_reason)
        if noauth_note:
            print("  NOTE: %s" % noauth_note)
    return 0


def cmd_set(args):
    # args.value is already constrained to VALID_STATES by argparse's own
    # choices= validation (raises Rejected -> handled in main() -> exit 0
    # unless --strict, exit 2). Nothing left to validate here.
    config_path = _cfg_path(args.repo)
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    cfg["state"] = args.value
    try:
        save_config(args.repo, cfg)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback set: failed to write config: %s\n" % exc)
        return 3 if args.strict else 0
    print("  config:                %s" % config_path)
    print("state set to '%s'" % args.value)
    return 0


def cmd_check(args):
    try:
        config_path = _cfg_path(args.repo)
        cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
        state = cfg["state"]
        # --role wins when given (explicit, deliberate -- lets an operator or a
        # test ask "would role X route?" without setting the env var); falls
        # back to HMD_AGENT_TYPE, the same variable bin/lib/hmd-route-claude's
        # own judgment pin reads, and the ONLY channel base-url's caller
        # (bin/heimdall-route, via _routing_decision) has at all. Irrelevant
        # outside state=="coop" -- run_preflight only ever consults it there.
        role = (getattr(args, "role", None) or "").strip() or (os.environ.get("HMD_AGENT_TYPE") or "").strip()
        checks = run_preflight(cfg, role=role)
        preflight_ok = all(ok for _, ok, _ in checks)
        session_verdict, session_window, session_windows_seen = (
            _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict(args.repo) if state == "auto" else (None, None, None)
        )
        verdict, rc = _verdict(state, checks, session_verdict=session_verdict)

        header = "heimdall-fallback check -- VERDICT: %s" % verdict
        if state == "switch":
            header += ("  *** SWITCH STATE: every task, every tier, routes to OmniRoute -- "
                       "capability tier is NOT consulted ***")
        print("  config:                %s" % config_path)
        print(header)
        if state == "coop":
            print("  role:                  %s" % (
                role or "(none identified -- coop_role_allowed will FAIL; pass --role or set HMD_AGENT_TYPE)"))

        for name, ok, reason in checks:
            line = "  [%s] %s" % ("OK  " if ok else "FAIL", name)
            if reason:
                line += " -- %s" % reason
            print(line)

        if state == "auto":
            note_text = (
                _session_usage_under_note(session_windows_seen)
                if session_verdict == "under" else SESSION_USAGE_NOTE_FMT[session_verdict]
            )
            info_line = "  [INFO] session_usage -- %s" % note_text
            if session_verdict == "crossed" and session_window:
                info_line += "  (window: %s)" % session_window
            print(info_line)

        if verdict == "WAIT" and not preflight_ok:
            print("  (state=auto: preflight failed -> waiting rather than routing blind)")
        elif verdict == "WAIT" and preflight_ok:
            print("  (state=auto: preflight passed but session_usage verdict is not "
                  "crossed -> waiting rather than routing while capacity remains)")
        elif verdict == "REFUSE" and state == "coop":
            print("  (state=coop: role is not routable -> refusing rather than silently leaving it unrouted)")
        elif verdict == "REFUSE" and state != "off":
            print("  (state=%s but a required check failed -> refusing rather than proceeding unsafely)" % state)

        note = _blocked_providers_note(args.repo)
        if note:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback check: WARNING: %s\n" % note)
        return rc
    except Exception as exc:
        # check() must fail CLOSED. Any internal error is indistinguishable
        # from "unsafe to route" -- refuse loudly, never fall through to a
        # bare success. See header's "MUST FAIL CLOSED".
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback check: internal error, refusing: %s\n" % exc)
        return 1


def _arm_validate_provider(provider, cfg):
    """Whether `provider` is something arm may EVER select or reuse as
    target_provider -- independent of run_preflight's own
    target_provider_allowed check (which only runs AFTER a value is already
    on disk). Never claude/claude-web (header point 1 -- arm enforces this
    itself since target_provider is never cross-checked against
    TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS anywhere else in this file) and never on the
    ToS-conflict deny list (header point 3)."""
    p = (provider or "").strip().lower()
    if not p:
        return False, "empty target_provider"
    if p in TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS:
        return False, (
            "'%s' is a Tier-1 subscription provider (claude/claude-web) -- arm never "
            "configures fallback to route to the operator's OWN Claude subscription; "
            "that is the exact boundary this whole tool exists to hold (header point 1)"
            % p
        )
    deny = BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS | {
        p2.strip().lower() for p2 in cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers", []) if isinstance(p2, str)
    }
    if p in deny:
        return False, "'%s' is on the ToS-conflict deny list (tos_flagged_providers)" % p
    return True, ""


def _arm_usable_key_env(cfg):
    """Whether cfg's OWN operator_key_env is already usable as-is: named,
    not a Claude/Anthropic marker (header point 4), and actually populated
    in this shell's environment. arm reuses this verbatim -- it never
    invents a credential."""
    key_env = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
    if not key_env:
        return None
    if any(marker in key_env.upper() for marker in FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS):
        return None
    if not os.environ.get(key_env):
        return None
    return key_env


def _arm_select_provider(args, cfg):
    """Pick (or validate) the target_provider + operator_key_env pair arm
    will write. Returns (provider, key_env, ok, reason); when ok is False,
    provider/key_env are both None and reason explains the refusal -- arm
    never arms a state it cannot justify."""
    noauth = _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg)

    requested = (args.provider or "").strip().lower()
    if requested:
        ok, reason = _arm_validate_provider(requested, cfg)
        if not ok:
            return None, None, False, reason
        if requested in noauth:
            return requested, "", True, ""
        key_env = _arm_usable_key_env(cfg)
        if key_env is not None:
            return requested, key_env, True, ""
        configured = cfg.get("operator_key_env") or ""
        if not configured:
            return None, None, False, (
                "'%s' is a keyed provider and needs operator_key_env -- arm never "
                "invents a credential. Set \"operator_key_env\": \"<YOUR_ENV_VAR_NAME>\" "
                "in %s to the NAME of an env var holding your OWN %s API key, export "
                "that var, then re-run arm" % (requested, _cfg_path(args.repo), requested)
            )
        if any(m in configured.upper() for m in FORBIDDEN_KEY_ENV_MARKERS):
            return None, None, False, (
                "operator_key_env '%s' names a Claude/Anthropic credential -- the "
                "Claude Code session can never be reused as a fallback key (header "
                "point 4)" % configured
            )
        return None, None, False, (
            "operator_key_env '%s' is configured but env var '%s' is not set in this "
            "shell -- export it, then re-run arm" % (configured, configured)
        )

    existing = (cfg.get("target_provider") or "").strip().lower()
    if existing:
        ok, _reason = _arm_validate_provider(existing, cfg)
        if ok:
            if existing in noauth:
                return existing, "", True, ""
            key_env = _arm_usable_key_env(cfg)
            if key_env is not None:
                return existing, key_env, True, ""
        # existing target_provider is denied, Tier-1, or keyed-without-a-usable-key
        # -- fall through to auto-picking a no-auth provider rather than refusing
        # outright.

    deny = BUILTIN_TOS_FLAGGED_PROVIDERS | {
        p.strip().lower() for p in cfg.get("tos_flagged_providers", []) if isinstance(p, str)
    }
    candidates = sorted(
        (p for p in noauth if p not in deny and p not in TIER1_DB_PROVIDERS),
        key=lambda p: (_arm_provider_rank(p), p),
    )
    if not candidates:
        return None, None, False, (
            "no honest no-auth provider is available to self-provision -- every known "
            "no-auth provider is denied by tos_flagged_providers. Specify --provider "
            "explicitly with a keyed provider (and a configured, populated "
            "operator_key_env) instead"
        )
    return candidates[0], "", True, ""


def cmd_arm(args):
    """Self-provision target_provider + operator_key_env and write state,
    then run the exact same preflight `check` runs and return its verdict --
    see header point 12. Never invents a credential, never touches
    claude/claude-web, never writes an unjustified state. Cannot perform two
    genuinely manual steps (exporting ANTHROPIC_MODEL into the PARENT shell,
    and starting the OmniRoute gateway itself) so it reports them instead of
    pretending either is already done."""
    config_path = _cfg_path(args.repo)
    cfg, corrupt_reason = load_config(args.repo)
    print("heimdall-fallback arm")
    print("  config:                %s" % config_path)
    if corrupt_reason:
        print("  NOTE: on-disk config was corrupt (%s) -- reset to safe defaults "
              "(state=off) before arming" % corrupt_reason)

    provider, key_env, ok, reason = _arm_select_provider(args, cfg)
    if not ok:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback arm: refusing -- %s\n" % reason)
        print("  REFUSED:               %s" % reason)
        print("  state left UNCHANGED (%s) -- arm never arms a state it cannot justify"
              % cfg.get("state"))
    else:
        is_noauth = provider in _resolve_noauth_providers(cfg)
        cfg["target_provider"] = provider
        cfg["operator_key_env"] = key_env
        cfg["state"] = args.state
        try:
            save_config(args.repo, cfg)
        except OSError as exc:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback arm: failed to write config: %s\n" % exc)
            print("  REFUSED:               failed to write config: %s" % exc)
            return 3 if args.strict else 0
        print("  target_provider:       %s (%s)" % (provider, "no-auth/keyless" if is_noauth else "keyed"))
        print("  operator_key_env:      %s" % (
            "(none needed -- keyless route)" if is_noauth
            else "%s (reused from an already-configured, already-populated env var -- "
                 "arm never invents a credential)" % key_env
        ))
        print("  state:                 %s" % args.state)

    endpoint = cfg.get("endpoint") or DEFAULT_CFG["endpoint"]
    if _is_local_endpoint(endpoint):
        if _endpoint_reachable(endpoint):
            print("  gateway:               reachable at %s" % endpoint)
        else:
            print("  gateway:               NOT REACHABLE at %s -- arm does not start "
                  "the OmniRoute gateway itself; start it, then re-run "
                  "'heimdall-fallback check'" % endpoint)
    else:
        print("  gateway:               endpoint '%s' is not a loopback address -- fix "
              "'endpoint' in %s" % (endpoint, config_path))

    model_provider = provider if ok else "<provider>"
    print()
    print("  ONE STEP ARM CANNOT DO FOR YOU -- run this yourself:")
    print("    export ANTHROPIC_MODEL=%s/<model-id>" % model_provider)
    print("    A child process (this command) cannot set an environment variable in")
    print("    the shell that launched it -- there is no syscall for that, so this")
    print("    line must be run BY YOU, in that shell (add it to your shell profile")
    print("    to persist it across sessions). Replace <model-id> with an actual")
    print("    model id the target provider serves -- check OmniRoute's own")
    print("    /v1/models listing; arm cannot know that id for you.")
    print()

    return cmd_check(args)


def _routing_decision(args):
    """The verdict, computed exactly as cmd_check computes it. Returns
    (rc, endpoint, reason). Shared so `base-url` can never drift from
    `check` -- two independent copies of a routing decision is how a gate
    starts saying ROUTE while the real gate says REFUSE.

    Role resolution here is deliberately HMD_AGENT_TYPE-only, never a
    --role flag: base-url is the seam bin/lib/hmd-route-claude's generation
    branch calls fresh on every spawn (via bin/heimdall-route), and neither
    of those callers ever passes one -- see header point 13. HMD_AGENT_TYPE
    set on a process survives exec automatically, so it is already present
    by the time this runs, with zero upstream changes needed. The MAIN
    agent identifies itself only by NOT carrying a coop-allowlisted
    HMD_AGENT_TYPE -- there is no separate, positive "this is the main
    session" marker, mirroring how bin/lib/hmd-route-claude's own
    _hrc_is_judgment() already treats an absent/non-matching HMD_AGENT_TYPE
    as "not judgment" with no affirmative signal of its own.
    """
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    role = (os.environ.get("HMD_AGENT_TYPE") or "").strip()
    checks = run_preflight(cfg, role=role)
    state = cfg["state"]
    session_verdict, _session_window, _session_windows_seen = (
        _session_pre_exhaustion_verdict(args.repo) if state == "auto" else (None, None, None)
    )
    verdict, rc = _verdict(state, checks, session_verdict=session_verdict)
    failed = [name for name, ok, _ in checks if not ok]
    reason = "verdict=%s (state=%s)" % (verdict, state)
    if failed:
        reason += "; failing checks: %s" % ", ".join(failed)
    elif state == "auto" and session_verdict != "crossed":
        reason += "; session_usage=%s (auto routes only on 'crossed')" % session_verdict
    return rc, (cfg.get("endpoint") or ""), reason


def cmd_base_url(args):
    """Print the OmniRoute base URL on stdout IFF the verdict is ROUTE, and
    print NOTHING to stdout otherwise -- the seam `hmd route` consumes in a
    command substitution.

    FAILS CLOSED for the same reason `check` does, and one stricter rule on
    top: stdout stays EMPTY on every non-ROUTE path, including an internal
    error. A caller does `url="$(heimdall-fallback base-url)"` and exports
    whatever it gets; a reason string, a traceback line or a partial verdict
    leaking onto stdout would be exported as a base URL and would silently
    point a live session at garbage. Reasons go to stderr, always."""
    try:
        rc, endpoint, reason = _routing_decision(args)
        if rc != 0:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback base-url: not routing -- %s\n" % reason)
            return rc
        if not endpoint or not _is_local_endpoint(endpoint):
            # Unreachable via the preflight (endpoint_local is a check), but a
            # base URL is too dangerous to emit on an assumption.
            sys.stderr.write(
                "heimdall-fallback base-url: refusing -- verdict is ROUTE but endpoint "
                "%r is not a loopback address\n" % endpoint)
            return 1
        sys.stdout.write("%s\n" % endpoint)
        return 0
    except Exception as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback base-url: refusing -- internal error: %s\n" % exc)
        return 1


def cmd_token_file(args):
    """Print the PATH of the gateway token file, never its contents.

    Refuses a file whose mode is group- or world-readable. A gateway token is
    a bearer credential for a surface that can reach third-party providers;
    handing back a path to a 0644 file would launder a real permission defect
    into a routed session. Silent on stdout whenever it refuses, same contract
    as base-url."""
    try:
        cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
        path = (cfg.get("gateway_token_file") or "").strip()
        if not path:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback token-file: no gateway_token_file configured\n")
            return 1
        path = os.path.abspath(os.path.expanduser(path))
        if not os.path.isfile(path):
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback token-file: %s does not exist\n" % path)
            return 1
        mode = os.stat(path).st_mode & 0o777
        if mode & 0o077:
            sys.stderr.write(
                "heimdall-fallback token-file: refusing %s -- mode %04o is group/world "
                "readable; a bearer token must be 0600 (chmod 600 it)\n" % (path, mode))
            return 1
        sys.stdout.write("%s\n" % path)
        return 0
    except Exception as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback token-file: refusing -- internal error: %s\n" % exc)
        return 1


def cmd_model(args):
    """Print the model id hmd should pin on a routed child, or NOTHING.

    Same stdout contract as base-url and for the same reason: `hmd route` reads
    this in a command substitution and exports the result as ANTHROPIC_MODEL. A
    reason string or a traceback on stdout would become the model id of a live
    session. Refuses any id _model_pin_reject_reason rejects -- notably a
    `claude/` or `claude-web/` prefix -- so this seam can never be the thing
    that points fallback traffic back at the operator's own subscription.
    """
    try:
        cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
        model = (cfg.get("fallback_model") or "").strip()
        if not model:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback model: no fallback_model configured\n")
            return 1
        reason = _model_pin_reject_reason(model, "fallback_model")
        if reason is not None:
            sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback model: refusing -- %s\n" % reason)
            return 1
        sys.stdout.write("%s\n" % model)
        return 0
    except Exception as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback model: refusing -- internal error: %s\n" % exc)
        return 1


def cmd_where(args):
    print(_cfg_path(args.repo))
    return 0


def cmd_coop_list(args):
    """Print the coop allowlist. Never-fail-caller telemetry read, matching
    status/where -- always exits 0."""
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    roles = cfg.get("coop_roles") or []
    if args.json:
        print(json.dumps({"coop_roles": roles}, sort_keys=True))
        return 0
    print("heimdall-fallback coop list")
    print("  config:                %s" % _cfg_path(args.repo))
    if not roles:
        print("  (empty -- coop routes NOTHING until a role is added; see 'coop add')")
    for role in roles:
        print("  %s" % role)
    return 0


def cmd_coop_add(args):
    """Add `role` to the coop allowlist. Refuses -- at CONFIG TIME, not just
    check time -- a judgment/adjudication role (header point 13): coop is a
    standing, deliberate allowlist, and a judgment role added to it today
    would silently start routing judgment traffic the moment
    hmd_is_adjudication's own classification is (correctly) trusted
    elsewhere, with no further action from the operator. Refusing here, on
    top of _coop_role_allowed's own check-time re-verification, means the
    unsafe entry can never even be written to disk in the first place.

    Exit 1 by default (2 under --strict) on refusal -- a deliberate
    departure from this file's usual "OSError only" default-0 pattern (see
    save_config's other callers): a deliberate safety refusal must be
    observable without requiring --strict, since real callers never pass it
    (see the top-level --strict help text) -- unlike a transient write
    failure, which stays default-0. See EXIT CODES above.
    """
    role = (args.role or "").strip()
    print("heimdall-fallback coop add")
    print("  config:                %s" % _cfg_path(args.repo))
    if not role:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback coop add: refusing -- empty/unresolvable role argument\n")
        print("  REFUSED:               empty/unresolvable role argument")
        return 2 if args.strict else 1
    if _is_adjudication_role(role):
        reason = ("'%s' is a judgment/adjudication role -- coop can never route judgment "
                   "traffic, at config time or check time (see bin/lib/hmd-adjudication-set.sh)" % role)
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback coop add: refusing -- %s\n" % reason)
        print("  REFUSED:               %s" % reason)
        return 2 if args.strict else 1
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    roles = set(cfg.get("coop_roles") or [])
    already_present = role in roles
    roles.add(role)
    cfg["coop_roles"] = sorted(roles)
    try:
        save_config(args.repo, cfg)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback coop add: failed to write config: %s\n" % exc)
        print("  REFUSED:               failed to write config: %s" % exc)
        return 3 if args.strict else 0
    if already_present:
        print("  '%s' is already on the coop allowlist" % role)
    else:
        print("  '%s' added to the coop allowlist" % role)
    return 0


def cmd_coop_remove(args):
    """Remove `role` from the coop allowlist. Removing a role that was
    never present is not an error -- the end state (role absent) is
    identical either way, so this is naturally idempotent and, unlike add,
    has no refusal path of its own: there is nothing unsafe about shrinking
    an allowlist. Exits 0 on a successful write, matching add/list's own
    success contract; a write failure mirrors set's 3-if-strict-else-0."""
    role = (args.role or "").strip()
    print("heimdall-fallback coop remove")
    print("  config:                %s" % _cfg_path(args.repo))
    cfg, _ = load_config(args.repo)
    roles = set(cfg.get("coop_roles") or [])
    was_present = role in roles
    roles.discard(role)
    cfg["coop_roles"] = sorted(roles)
    try:
        save_config(args.repo, cfg)
    except OSError as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback coop remove: failed to write config: %s\n" % exc)
        print("  REFUSED:               failed to write config: %s" % exc)
        return 3 if args.strict else 0
    if was_present:
        print("  '%s' removed from the coop allowlist" % role)
    else:
        print("  '%s' was not on the coop allowlist (nothing to remove)" % role)
    return 0


def cmd_coop(args):
    """Dispatch coop's own nested subcommand (add/remove/list).
    build_parser wires coop_cmd as required, so args.coop_cmd is always one
    of these three -- argparse itself rejects anything else before this
    ever runs."""
    handlers = {
        "add": cmd_coop_add,
        "remove": cmd_coop_remove,
        "list": cmd_coop_list,
    }
    return handlers[args.coop_cmd](args)


def build_parser():
    p = _Parser(
        prog="heimdall-fallback",
        description="Quota-exhaustion fallback policy gate (state/config/safety only -- no transport).",
    )
    p.add_argument("--repo", default=".", help="repo root containing .heimdall/ (default: .)")
    p.add_argument("--strict", action="store_true", help="tests only; callers never pass this")
    sub = p.add_subparsers(dest="cmd", required=True)

    sp_status = sub.add_parser("status", help="current state + whether preflight would pass (secret-free)")
    sp_status.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")

    sp_set = sub.add_parser("set", help="persist a new fallback state")
    sp_set.add_argument("value", choices=VALID_STATES)

    sp_check = sub.add_parser("check", help="run the preflight; exit code is the verdict (0=ROUTE 1=REFUSE 2=WAIT)")
    sp_check.add_argument(
        "--role", default=None,
        help="subagent role to evaluate under state=coop (default: read HMD_AGENT_TYPE "
             "from the environment); ignored under every other state. A bare check with "
             "no resolvable role under coop always REFUSEs -- never a silent ROUTE",
    )

    sp_arm = sub.add_parser(
        "arm",
        help="self-provision target_provider + operator_key_env (never inventing a "
             "credential) and write state, then run check and report its verdict",
    )
    sp_arm.add_argument(
        "--provider", default=None,
        help="explicit target_provider to validate and use (default: reuse an "
             "already-valid configured one, else auto-pick a no-auth provider)",
    )
    sp_arm.add_argument(
        "--state", default="auto", choices=("auto", "switch"),
        help="fallback state to write once provisioning succeeds (default: auto); coop "
             "is deliberately not an arm target -- it is a hand-curated allowlist, never "
             "something to auto-provision (see 'coop add')",
    )

    sub.add_parser(
        "base-url",
        help="print the OmniRoute base URL iff the verdict is ROUTE (stdout stays "
             "empty otherwise); the seam `hmd route` consumes",
    )

    sub.add_parser(
        "token-file",
        help="print the PATH (never the contents) of the configured gateway token "
             "file, refusing a path that is not 0600",
    )

    sub.add_parser(
        "model",
        help="print the model id hmd pins on a routed child (stdout stays empty if "
             "none is configured or the configured one is unsafe)",
    )

    sub.add_parser("where", help="print the config file path")

    sp_coop = sub.add_parser(
        "coop",
        help="manage the coop per-role allowlist (state=coop routes ONLY these roles; "
             "empty by default, judgment roles can never be added)",
    )
    coop_sub = sp_coop.add_subparsers(dest="coop_cmd", required=True)

    sp_coop_add = coop_sub.add_parser("add", help="add a role to the coop allowlist")
    sp_coop_add.add_argument("role", help="subagent role name, e.g. hmd:coder")

    sp_coop_remove = coop_sub.add_parser("remove", help="remove a role from the coop allowlist")
    sp_coop_remove.add_argument("role", help="subagent role name, e.g. hmd:coder")

    sp_coop_list = coop_sub.add_parser("list", help="print the current coop allowlist")
    sp_coop_list.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")

    return p


def main(argv):
    parser = build_parser()
    strict = "--strict" in argv
    try:
        args = parser.parse_args(argv)
    except Rejected as exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback: %s\n" % exc)
        return 2 if strict else 0
    args.repo = os.path.abspath(args.repo)
    handlers = {
        "status": cmd_status,
        "set": cmd_set,
        "check": cmd_check,
        "arm": cmd_arm,
        "base-url": cmd_base_url,
        "token-file": cmd_token_file,
        "model": cmd_model,
        "where": cmd_where,
        "coop": cmd_coop,
    }
    return handlers[args.cmd](args)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    _argv = sys.argv[1:]
    _strict = "--strict" in _argv
    try:
        sys.exit(main(_argv))
    except BrokenPipeError:
        sys.exit(0)
    except SystemExit:
        raise
    except Exception as _exc:
        sys.stderr.write("heimdall-fallback: unexpected error: %s\n" % _exc)
        sys.exit(3 if _strict else 0)
